**Step 1**: Create the configuration directory
First, make sure the OpenCode folder exists in your system's config directory. Run this in your terminal:

```mkdir -p ~/.config/opencode```

**Step 2**: Open the configuration file
Open (or create) the opencode.json file using the nano text editor:

`nano ~/.config/opencode/opencode.json`

**Step 3**: Paste the configuration
Copy the JSON block below and paste it into the terminal. This block does three things: it sets up Minimax as the provider, passes in your API key to fix that Anthropic error, and defines the new token-plan model names.

```
JSON
{
  "provider": {
    "minimax": {
      "options": {
        "apiKey": "YOUR_MINIMAX_API_KEY_HERE"
      },
      "models": {
        "minimax-token-plan": {},
        "minimax-cn-token-plan": {}
      }
    }
  },
  "model": "minimax/minimax-token-plan"
}
```
(Make sure to replace "YOUR_MINIMAX_API_KEY_HERE" with your actual key before saving!)

**Step 4**: Save and exit
To save the file in nano:

Press Ctrl + O (that's the letter O, not zero) to write the changes.
Press Enter to confirm the file name.
Press Ctrl + X to exit the editor.

**Step 5:** Restart OpenCode
Close any existing OpenCode sessions you have running in your terminal, and then simply launch it again by typing:

OpenCode will now read this file on startup, authenticate correctly with Minimax, and use the updated minimax-token-plan model. You can select Minimax 2.7 as well, it will work too.

@rekram1-node thanks for the hunt with `opencode debug...`

I think I had an issue on my end with an additional wildcard in my `opencode.json` that allowed the MCP tools again. 

So I assume it was a layer 8 issue. I'll do some more tests and then might close the issue, adding more documentation here

```
> This seems to be an issue where the metadata on models.dev isn't setup correctly. This can be overwritten for now with a custom model config until they push the correction.
> 
> {
>   "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
>   "provider": {
>     "opencode-go": {
>       "models": {
>         "qwen3.6-plus": {
>           "limit": {
>             "context": 1000000,
>             "output": 65536
>           }
>         }
>       }
>     }
>   }
> }
```

Indeed, expliciltly declaring the context window limit bypassed the problem, which can also add some models supported by corresponding APIs but not in Opencode. Now we shall wait until further official notification.
